"""Oxalate reference data compiled from the Harvard School of Public Health oxalate table.

Source: Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health, Renal and Urology News Oxalate Table
URL: https://hsph.harvard.edu/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/OXALATE-TABLE-1.xlsx
Date retrieved: 2026-06-22
Original data credit: Dr John Knight (University of Alabama School of Medicine)

Each record has:
  group                   — food category from Harvard table
  food_name               — food name (title-cased from original)
  serving_size            — serving size description from Harvard table
  oxalate_mg_per_serving  — mg oxalate per listed serving
  oxalate_mg_per_100g     — mg oxalate per 100g (None when serving is volumetric/count)
  category                — "very high" | "high" | "moderate" | "low" | "negligible"
                            based on per_100g when available, per_serving thresholds otherwise
  directly_measured       — True if Harvard table marks value as directly measured (*)

Category thresholds (per 100g):  >=300 very high, >=100 high, >=25 moderate, >=5 low, else negligible
Category thresholds (per serving, when no 100g available):
    >=100 very high, >=26 high, >=10 moderate, >=2 low, else negligible
"""

